<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Tambapanni</h1><p class="st-meta">543 BCE – 437 BCE (approx.) · capital Tambapanni</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first kingdom of Sri Lanka in the island's chronicles, founded by the legendary prince Vijaya on the north-west coast.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dipavamsa">Dipavamsa</a> chronicles tell how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-vijaya">Prince Vijaya</a>, exiled from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, landed with 700 followers on the day of the Buddha's death, traditionally 543 BCE. He married Kuveni, a local woman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yakkha-sri-lanka">Yakkha</a> people, and founded a settlement called Tambapanni, probably near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannar">Mannar</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puttalam">Puttalam</a>. The account is legendary, though it may reflect the arrival of Indo-Aryan speakers.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nothing is known of the kingdom from contemporary sources. The chronicles say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-vijaya">Vijaya</a> later sent Kuveni away and married a princess from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> country, and that his followers founded villages across the north of the island. Archaeology shows early iron-using settlements in the region, notably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>, that predate the chronicles' kings.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>According to the chronicles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-vijaya">Vijaya</a>'s nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panduvasudeva">Panduvasudeva</a> moved the seat of rule to Upatissa Gama, and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandukabhaya">Pandukabhaya</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> around 437 BCE, beginning the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/anuradhapura-kingdom">Anuradhapura Kingdom</a>. The Greek name for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taprobane">Taprobane</a>, derives from Tambapanni.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-tambapanni.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Tambapanni" width="320" height="446"><figcaption>Image: Yixopeh377 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Tambapanni.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>543 BCE – 437 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tambapanni</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sinhala Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tambapanni, Kingdom of Vijaya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-490&amp;focus=kingdom-of-tambapanni" aria-label="Kingdom of Tambapanni on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6908 1898 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Tambapanni lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7222 2122h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7228 2154h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7228 2154h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Sri Lanka. <a href="/atlas?year=-490&amp;focus=kingdom-of-tambapanni">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
